WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.400 position:63%
My guest this week is the primatologist,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.020
Dr. Jane Goodall.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.700
Famed for her work on noting

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.610
the connection between
chimpanzees and humans,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.930
she now travels the world

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.900
with her educational
program and her message.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.210
Next stop is Arizona.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.850
Aged 88, she's not slowing down

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.990
and she describes herself now
as an environmental activist,

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.000 position:58%
but don't expect her to be lying down

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.033
in the middle of a road and
stopping traffic anytime soon.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.660
Jane, welcome to the program.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.740
Thank you so much for being
with me and with all of us.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.230 position:63%
You made some very important discoveries

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.080 position:66%
about chimpanzees early on in your career,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.790
which really made your name,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.560
and I wanted to start
our conversation today

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.400
by asking what do you
think we need to remember

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.790
about what you've learned and
what's been discovered since

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:06.390
about our similarities
as species, perhaps?

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.790 position:59%
Well, I think the most important thing

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.250
that I can say is that, you know,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.970
genetically the composition
of DNA of humans

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.710
and chimpanzees differs
by only just over 1%.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.460
So biologically, they're
our closest living relative,

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.170
along with the bonobo,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.870 position:66%
and then behaviorally, kissing, embracing,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.003
holding hands, patting one another,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.210
the relationship between mothers

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.890
and their growing offspring,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.810
which can last throughout a life

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.991 position:56%
of up to about 65 years in the wild.

00:01:39.991 --> 00:01:44.340
And unfortunately, they have a dark

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.190
and aggressive side, just like us,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.030
and they're capable of a kind
of primitive war and killing.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.850 position:58%
But just like us, they have a loving,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.270 position:63%
compassionate and truly altruistic side.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.788 position:58%
So in all those ways, it's very clear

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.860
and became clear to scientists

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.530 position:61%
that we are part of the animal kingdom,

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:12.530
and not as was said in the early
'60s, completely separated,

00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:16.710 position:64%
and the chimpanzees really have helped us

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.410 position:59%
to understand our position in relation

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.440
to the rest of the animals
that we are part of

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.410 position:64%
and not separate from the animal kingdom.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.930
And is that the point of trying

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.720
to understand how we are similar,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.880 position:63%
that we don't see ourselves as distinct?

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.310
Why is that important?

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.180
Well, because once people decide

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:42.900
that animals are mere
things, as was thought,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.300
then the way that they're treated

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.240
can be extremely inhumane.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.070 position:59%
And as we know now, we're in the midst

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.140
of the sixth great extinction

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.860
and in many places, animal
lives are not valued.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.470
But then we have to remember
that in many places,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.290
human lives are not valued either.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:05.033
And I think the one thing
that we have to think about

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:08.670 position:64%
is that the biggest difference between us

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.040
and chimpanzees and other animals

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.550
is the explosive development
of our intellect.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.220
So it doesn't make sense, does it,

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.050
that the most intellectual creature

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:24.050
to ever walk on planet earth
is destroying its only home.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.330
And it seems to me
there is some disconnect

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.890
between the clever, clever
brain and the human heart

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:35.760
where poetically we seek
love and compassion.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.430
And I truly believe when
you, in head and heart,

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.200
work in harmony, can we humans
attain our true potential.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.540
And our true potential is huge.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.360
Just to go to that point of love

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.930
and our similarity with chimpanzees

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.080
when it comes to our
relationships, there is this video,

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.350
this recent footage of a
baby chimp that's gone viral.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.760
The baby had to be put on oxygen

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.800
for a few days after being born,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.870 position:64%
and it's a video of it being reintroduced

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.940
to its mother for the first time.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.100 position:59%
I know you've had a look at this clip.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.630
It's really moving.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.740
I mean, I say that as someone

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.660
who's nearly eight
months pregnant as well,

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.270
but just watching the interaction

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.060
when they're reunited with
the mother, how quick she is.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.443
What did you take from it?

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:24.330 position:63%
Well, I was very moved by watching that.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.700 position:59%
And clearly, when the mother comes in,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.650
she's not sure what's happening.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.590
She'd been separated
from her baby at birth.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:34.020
I believe it was a cesarean.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.440 position:66%
And then suddenly, you see the infant move

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.300
and the arm reaches up,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.080
and the reaction of the
rapid response of the mother,

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.660
the speed with which she moves over

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.590
and just so tenderly cradles
that infant in her arms,

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:52.440
and she's so excited that
she's kind of rocking,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.630 position:63%
but very gently because she doesn't want

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:59.130
to disturb this precious little
life that she has created.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.860
And I think it gives us a window

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.050
into the chimpanzee mentality,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.700 position:63%
the way that she just relates, as I say,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.030 position:58%
to that newborn infant is incredible.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.710
Does it bring back
memories, good memories,

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.110 position:66%
for you about working with the chimpanzees

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.620
and being with them every day
and studying them up close?

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.490
Well, during those amazing
years, the best of my life,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.580 position:66%
I was learning something almost every day.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.550 position:59%
And I saw the whole gamut of emotions,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.880 position:58%
you know, the loving and caring side,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.030 position:64%
and also the violent and aggressive side,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.240
I saw the tenderness of mothers

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.170
and the older siblings
towards newborn babies,

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:47.370
I also saw some females who
cannibalize newborn babies

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.530
of other females in their group,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.860 position:64%
which still doesn't make any sense to us.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.860 position:64%
And so when you are watching chimpanzees,

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.910
you run through the same
contradictions in behavior

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.640
that we see when we are
looking at human beings,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.600
because we too behave
in unpredictable ways.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.300
Well, I know that you
have focused your work

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.010
of late more away from animals

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.230 position:63%
towards people, towards the environment.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.213
Why did you make that move?

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:20.400 position:64%
Because in 1986, I helped to put together

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.860
a conference in Chicago actually,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.590 position:66%
and by that time, there were six different

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:29.670 position:61%
field study sites studying chimpanzees.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.200
When I began, it was just me.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.030
But by 1986, there were other
people out there in the field.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.710
And the aim of it was to see

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.770 position:66%
how does chimpanzee behavior differ or not

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.530
in one environment to another.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.680
But during that four day conference

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.150
in which we learned a lot
about chimpanzee culture,

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.300 position:59%
we also had a session on conservation.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.710
And it was absolutely shocking

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.960 position:56%
to see right across the chimp range,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.490
chimp numbers were dropping,

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.890
forests were being destroyed.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.320
It was the beginning
of the bush meat trade;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.690
killing mothers to steal babies

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.450
for the international
trade in wild animals

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.890
for entertainment zoos,

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.800 position:63%
and in those days, for medical research.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.220 position:59%
By then, I'd magically obtained my PhD

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.010 position:64%
in spite of never having been to college.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.200
But I left as an activist.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:24.510
And we're talking now just after

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.840 position:61%
the latest COP 27 Global Climate summit

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.420
of most of the world's leaders.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.280
Am I finding you in an optimistic

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.400
or a pessimistic place after that?

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.260
Well, I've attended some

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:40.686 position:63%
of those COP Climate Change conferences,

00:07:40.686 --> 00:07:44.700
and, you know, if people,
if countries live up

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.317
to the pledges that they make

00:07:46.317 --> 00:07:49.350 position:63%
and the commitments that they talk about

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.100
and the eloquent way that
they say they understand

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:58.100
that we must keep the rise
in temperature under 1.5,

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.350
and I've heard some people
now saying two degrees,

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.780
if these pledges were kept,

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.580
but there has been a history

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.030
of pledges being made and not kept.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:13.440
So all I can say is I
have my fingers crossed.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.500
There's no question
awareness about the danger

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:19.200
of climate change and
loss of biodiversity,

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.300
because these two go together

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.730
and, you know, we depend
on the natural world

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:27.000
for food, water,
clothing, air, everything,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.940 position:56%
but we depend on healthy ecosystems.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.020
And an ecosystem is made
up of this complex mix

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.360
of animal and plant species.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.550
It's like a glorious tapestry,

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.740
but they're all interrelated.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.510
So as one species becomes extinct
in a particular ecosystem,

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.090
it's like pulling a
thread from the tapestry.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.100
And if enough threads are pulled,

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.800
the tapestry will hang in tatters

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.200
and the ecosystem will collapse.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.850
I mean, you say you have
your fingers crossed.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.780
I know you like to be an
optimist, from all you've said,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:04.230
and you actually say
that's a key part of going

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.150
and keeping going with
the work that you do.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.970
But say you've got your fingers
crossed after this meeting,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.350
it doesn't sound that hopeful.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.060 position:63%
I mean, you sound like you want to hope,

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:13.710 position:56%
but not necessarily that you believe

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:16.230 position:61%
these leaders will keep to the pledges.

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.350
Well, that is where we move away

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.170 position:63%
from the leaders and turn to the people.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.660
So that my main reasons for hope,

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.460
we, the Jane Goodall Institute,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:28.860
we have a program for young people

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.370
from kindergarten through
university and beyond,

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.815
it's in 66 countries and growing,

00:09:35.815 --> 00:09:38.520 position:61%
hundreds and thousands of young people,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.890
they care, they get it.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:45.240
Many, many of them are taking
action about climate change

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.470
and all of these issues that
are so devastating at the time.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:50.520
But they care.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.277
And they not only care
and say that they care,

00:09:53.277 --> 00:09:56.040
but they roll up their
sleeves and they take action,

00:09:56.040 --> 00:10:00.210
because they get to choose the
projects that they work on.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:02.400 position:64%
And they work on projects to help people,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.040
to help animals and to
help the environment.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.270 position:63%
I was gonna say, if you're watching this

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.280 position:59%
and you haven't rolled up your sleeves

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.290
and maybe you're out
of the education system

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.780 position:58%
and you're thinking, "What can I do?"

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.280
What's the one thing you think
more people could do easily?

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.150
Well, easily, they could move
towards a plant-based diet.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:24.481
Easily, they could try
and eliminate plastics

00:10:24.481 --> 00:10:27.330
from their lives.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.119
Easily, they could...

00:10:30.119 --> 00:10:32.610
For different people, it's
different things, you know?

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:37.610
Some people want to help
immigrant children, for example.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.840
And because it's all interrelated,

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.180
all these problems we face today,

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:44.760
we need people to tackle them all.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.540
So what I feel very, very strongly,

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.270 position:64%
yes, there is indeed hope for the future,

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.915
but there's a but,

00:10:52.915 --> 00:10:54.570
there's a window of time

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.990 position:58%
when we can slow down climate change,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.050 position:59%
we can slow down loss of biodiversity,

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:05.220
we can improve healthcare,
we can alleviate poverty,

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.070 position:59%
which is a great driver of destruction

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.650 position:64%
of the environment as people try to live.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.980
But there's a but, and
the but is we can do this,

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.290
but we all need to take action.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:18.990 position:56%
We must stop relying on governments.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.280
And the good news,

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:23.200
the big corporations
are beginning to change.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.230
Jane, what do you say to people

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:29.640
who say things like, "Well,
while big countries like China

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.010
are not meeting these
sorts of commitments,

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:33.000
these sorts of pledges,

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:36.540
not changing their economies
enough to be different.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.460
I know you're talking about
coming away from leaders.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.450
"I'm not gonna bother.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.150 position:66%
It makes no difference if I don't recycle.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.940
It makes no difference
if I eat less meat."

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:45.900
What do you say to those people

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.370
who don't have that optimism,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.980
because they look at the
countries and the leaders

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.800 position:58%
who are not doing what you're saying?

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:53.910
Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:56.430 position:63%
And isn't there an expression that's all

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.970
over the place saying, "Think
globally, act locally."

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:01.980
It's the wrong way around.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:04.590 position:66%
Because if you think globally, as you say,

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:07.920
we get depressed and
you cannot look around

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:09.840 position:61%
at what's happening in the world today,

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.180 position:61%
politically, socially, environmentally,

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:15.000
and not feel a deep depression.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.470
But what I say to those people
is stop thinking globally.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.870
You can't do anything globally,

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:23.580
but think in your own community.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.100 position:59%
What can I do? What do you care about?

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.920
Go inspire others, get to work,

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.780
roll up your sleeves, do something.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.660
And that makes people feel good.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.460
I wanted to ask, you said
you left that meeting

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.650
in the '80s, an activist.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:40.170
There are a new generation,
as there always are,

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.570
but there are at the moment
of environmental activists,

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:44.700 position:61%
for instance, in the UK, Just Stop Oil,

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:47.250
the Sunrise Movement in America.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.340 position:59%
There are mixed views of that as a way

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.280
of trying to change people's
minds and the messages.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:55.560
Of course, in some instances
people have been stopped

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.410
from going to medical
appointments, funerals,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.540
they've had their lives disrupted.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:02.310 position:58%
That's what protests are meant to do.

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:05.580 position:58%
How do you view that type of activism

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.590 position:59%
at the moment that you'll be aware of?

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.960
I think it's a huge mistake.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:13.971 position:66%
It's alienating people who otherwise care.

00:13:13.971 --> 00:13:18.971
It makes them angry and it
tends to make people think

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:22.870
about all of us who care
about climate change

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.910
in a very negative way,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.360
or sort of branded like
the old tree hugger type.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.470
And so, them, I can
understand their anger,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:36.480
but I cannot condone their methods.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.110
I mean, here you have
maybe hundreds and hundreds

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.750
of people who are doing their best,

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:44.970
trying to live lives
that are more in harmony

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:48.060
with the natural world and
they can't get home to supper,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:49.920
they can't pick up their children,

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.500
because you've got these
ridiculous activists

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:55.050 position:63%
blocking the road or chaining themselves

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.910
to posts and things like that.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:00.360 position:58%
So for me, that is not the right way.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.040
I can understand their anger,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:04.830
but it's misdirected, I believe.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:07.650
Would you go as far as
calling those activists

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.950
as some politicians have,
would you call them criminals?

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:13.530
No, I wouldn't go so far
as to call them criminals.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:18.430
They're just, I don't know,
they're thinking wrongly

00:14:20.336 --> 00:14:21.990
and that's the problem.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.180 position:58%
You know, if you think of our species

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.940
at the mouth of a very
long, very dark tunnel,

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:29.160
and right at the end there's
a little shining light,

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:30.300
that's hope.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:32.910 position:59%
And between us and that shining light,

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:37.050
there are all these obstacles,
the ones that I've mentioned.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.510
So it's no good sitting
at the mouth of the tunnel

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.000
and saying, "I hope that
light comes to us soon."

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.670
No, no, no, we have to
roll up those sleeves

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.460
and crawl under, climb
over and work around

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:52.320
all those obstacles lying
between us and the star.

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:56.130
And yes, there are people
working on every single one

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.860
of the problems mentioned
and not mentioned,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.990
but too often they tend to work

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.180
in their own little narrow tunnel.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.790
And if you don't see
the picture as a whole,

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.371
you may succeed in your goal,

00:15:08.371 --> 00:15:11.700 position:59%
but that may cause problems elsewhere.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.350 position:66%
Can I ask you a very, very random question

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:14.183
just at this point?

00:15:14.183 --> 00:15:16.950
Is it right what I've read
that you were presented

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.810
as a debutante at Buckingham Palace

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:22.050
at the last one of those
kind of events held there?

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:22.883
Is that right?

00:15:22.883 --> 00:15:27.750 position:58%
At a debutante, yes, because my uncle

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.450
who had money wanted me presented.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:33.720
We had no money, we had to
scrounge around, Mum and I,

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:38.460
in secondhand shops to find
something suitable to wear.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:43.380
And I was lined up with
all these other debutantes,

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:44.490
ready for the season,

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.260
which basically a marriage market.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:47.093
Yeah.

00:15:47.093 --> 00:15:48.900
And they were saying to me

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:50.550
that their ambition was to be

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:52.650
a lady in waiting to the Queen.

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.510 position:61%
When I said, no, I want to go to Africa

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.730
and live with wild
animals, they avoided me.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.316
I mean, it was like I was
shunned like a pariah.

00:15:59.316 --> 00:16:02.370
Well it's quite a memory, I'm sure,

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:05.220
'cause I mean, you used to
travel with your mum, didn't you?

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:07.530
Well, only because, I mean,

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.010
the first time I went to
Africa, I was on my own.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.640
And then when Louis Leakey
asked me to study the chimps,

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.580
the Tanzania, was Tanganyika,

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:20.130 position:58%
part of the crumbling British Empire,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:24.870
and the British authorities
refused permission

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:28.110 position:61%
to take responsibility for a young girl

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.390
going out into the forest alone,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:31.980
they said, "No, no, no, no, no,"

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.190
and in the end, they said, "Yes,
but she cannot come alone,"

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:37.740
and Mum volunteered to come.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:39.180
Wow, I bet that was quite,

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.520
I mean, I bet that was quite
something for her as well.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:42.420
Well she was...

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.660 position:59%
People say I was brave. She was brave.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.553
She stayed down in the camp,

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:50.910
there was spiders and scorpions

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:54.030
and snakes and buffaloes came by,

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:57.420
and we had one cook and
he very quickly found

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:01.680
a frightfully alcoholic beverage
made of fermented bananas.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.130
So he was slightly
inebriated half the time.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.110
I was up in the hills,

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:09.990
doing what I dreamed of
doing since I was 10.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:10.823
That's amazing.

00:17:10.823 --> 00:17:13.110
That's amazing that she
supported you like that

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:14.220
and that you were able to do that.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:15.450
Oh, she was.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.190
I wouldn't be who I am.

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:20.220
I mean, when I was 10 and
had this dream of Africa,

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.290
'cause I was in love with Tarzan,

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:23.737
everybody laughed and said,

00:17:23.737 --> 00:17:25.020
"You'll never get to Africa.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.120 position:58%
It's far away. You don't have money,"

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:30.960
World War II was raging
and, "You're just a girl."

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:32.617
But what my mother said was,

00:17:32.617 --> 00:17:34.740
"If you really want to do this,

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.900
you're going to have
to work terribly hard,

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.330 position:56%
take advantage of every opportunity,

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.690
and then if you don't give
up, hopefully you find a way."

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:46.080
And that message, I've
told to young people

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:46.950
all around the world,

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.560 position:66%
particularly in disadvantaged communities,

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:53.437
and I wish Mum was around to
hear how many people have said,

00:17:53.437 --> 00:17:55.260
"Jane, I want to thank you,

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:58.440
because you've taught me
that because you did it,

00:17:58.440 --> 00:17:59.910
I can do it too."

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:00.990
That's incredible.

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:01.823
It's really incredible.

00:18:01.823 --> 00:18:04.350 position:64%
I mean, and, you know, you were very rare

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:07.680
as a woman, as a young
woman in that space,

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.360
but I've read you also found that

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.680
to your advantage in some ways.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:11.910
You've taken it in good humor,

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:13.740 position:64%
especially when some comments, I believe,

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:16.487 position:63%
were made about your legs being helpful.

00:18:16.487 --> 00:18:20.340 position:66%
Well, back then, it was a different world.

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:23.347 position:64%
Like if somebody made that comment today,

00:18:23.347 --> 00:18:26.340
"Oh, Jane only got onto
the geographic cover

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:28.947 position:66%
and famous and money because of her legs,"

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.700 position:64%
it would probably be cause for a lawsuit.

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:35.310
Back then, I just said, "Well,

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:37.890
if indeed my legs have got me money

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:41.640 position:66%
to study the chimpanzees, thank you legs!"

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.560
It's probably the only way
to deal with it back then.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.210 position:56%
Like you say, where things were said

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:47.010 position:59%
that would not be said in the same way

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.343
or certainly received in the
same way as they are now.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:54.720
I also liked, just reading
in terms of your life

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:56.970 position:63%
and your ability to connect with people,

00:18:56.970 --> 00:19:00.390
that pre pandemic, is it
right you hadn't slept

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:05.110
in the same bed for more
than three weeks since 1986

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:07.560
because of your travel commitments,

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.440
meeting friends and
people around the world?

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:12.270
That's absolutely true.

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:14.520
And because during the pandemic,

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:16.650 position:58%
I was grounded at home in Bournemouth

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:19.110 position:63%
where I'm speaking from Bournemouth now,

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:21.420 position:61%
and it's the longest I've been at home.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.640 position:63%
It's the house I grew up in, by the way,

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.910 position:66%
where me and my sister are and her family.

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:30.000
It's the longest I've been
there since I left school.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.000
But it was more exhausting
than all that traveling,

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:38.013 position:56%
because I could do up to four Zooms,

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:41.610
virtual lectures and interviews

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:44.070
and things like this every day.

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:47.340 position:58%
My voice got exhausted, it's changed,

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.490
and you know, there was no
respite, there was no day off,

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:54.570
not one in two and a
half years, no holiday.

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:59.310 position:63%
And so the advantage was that I was able

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.590
to reach literally millions,

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:04.980 position:61%
several million more people in that way

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:09.090
than by traveling and talking
in the different countries.

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:12.210 position:61%
And so the idea was after the pandemic,

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:16.680
we'll do more of the static
Zooming and less of the travel.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:20.070
Well, it turns out that
I'm doing as much travel

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:24.330 position:61%
and the Zooms, so I'm doubly exhausted.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:27.240
You doubled up there
with some of the load.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.398
What do you make about travel

00:20:29.398 --> 00:20:31.350 position:59%
as that being one of the things people

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:32.640
are trying to cut down

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:34.800 position:66%
or one of the things they try and control?

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:36.570
How do you reconcile that?

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:39.900
Well, first of all, our
Roots &amp; Shoots groups

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:43.770
are planting literally
millions and millions of trees.

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:47.997
Secondly, if my arrival in a place

00:20:47.997 --> 00:20:52.320
didn't make a big
difference, I wouldn't do it.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:53.220
But it does.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:55.440 position:59%
So many people have said, "Well, Jane,

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:57.780 position:63%
it's wonderful when you Zoom and things,

00:20:57.780 --> 00:20:59.790
but when you come, we get the kind

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.970
of inspiration we don't
get in any other way."

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.490
So although I'm on these planes,

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:07.380
I'm not on a private plane,

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.050
I'm on a plane that's going anyway.

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:14.280
We haven't got to the stage
